! conclusion: This is a problem-stating node: a fundamental gap (the missing Q(o|pi) constructor) sat always-renderable in the audit and never blocking, because the letter-number backlog and keyword-statuses drowned it out.

  + context: Minted by library-loop row L5 from EPIC-run-era.md; it exists so @why edges from fundamentals-gating patterns have a problem node to land on.

  + IF:
    A queue of individually-validated tasks competes with a fundamental gap that blocks them all.

  + HOWEVER:
    The problem, per the ruling (Joe, verbatim, 2026-09-05): "the missing Q(o|π) constructor our interface audit keeps finding.... this is the kind of fundamental gap that the big backlog of letter-number tasks and :keyword-statuses was drowning out. this should be a major finding and a priority focus, the Lean model should be saying stop other work until this is solved (and similar needed fundamentals exist)". The finding had been in the audit since the table was first built — always renderable, never blocking; the failure was the queue's, not the audit's. (source: futon2 holes labs wm-contract EPIC-run-era.md, The fundamentals ruling, 2026-09-05)

  + THEN:
    Treat this node as the landing point for @why edges from patterns that answer fundamentals-drowning; solving it is the :F class's job, not this node's.

  + BECAUSE:
    Without a refusing priority, every individually-validated task outranks the gap that invalidates them all.
    + evidence: futon2 holes labs wm-contract EPIC-run-era.md, "## The fundamentals ruling — the Lean model gates the queue (Joe, 2026-09-05)".
